/-
ThirtySevenKernelPolynomials.lean — own work for the Fermat project (not vendored).

**The two explicit kernel-polynomial certificates at level `37`**, cut
2026-07-30 out of the sorry leaf
`MazurIsogenyPrimeJ.exists_isogenyCurve_thirtySeven` in
`Fermat/FLT/FreyCurve/MazurTorsion.lean`.

## What this file does

`Fermat/FLT/EllipticCurve/KernelPolynomial.lean` reduces "`E/ℚ` carries a
Galois-stable cyclic subgroup of prime order `p`" to a purely polynomial
certificate `WeierstrassCurve.IsKernelPolynomial E p f m`, and
`Fermat/FLT/EllipticCurve/GenusOneKernelPolynomials.lean` supplies six such
certificates at `p ∈ {11, 17, 19}`.  This file does the same at `p = 37`, for
the two rows of `MazurIsogenyPrimeJ.thirtySevenJTable` — the Mazur–Swinnerton-Dyer
pair of conductor `1225 = 5²·7²`:

| `j₀` | model `[a₁,a₂,a₃,a₄,a₆]` | `m` |
|------|--------------------------|-----|
| `−9317 = −7·11³` | `[1, 1, 1, -8, 6]` | 2 |
| `−162677523113838677 = −7·137³·2083³` | `[1, 1, 0, -284865655, -1850700345850]` | 2 |

`2` is a primitive root modulo `37` (`znprimroot(37) = 2`), so the `generates`
field is satisfiable at both rows with the same multiplier.

**Why the second model is NOT the minimal one.**  The minimal model at that
`j` is `[1, 1, 1, -208083, -36621194]` (conductor `1225`, `Δ = -6125`), and its
kernel polynomial is **not integral**: its constant coefficient has a
denominator of `37`, because the `37`-torsion points cutting out the kernel have
`x`-coordinates of `37`-adic valuation `−1` there.  Every coefficient below
would then be a rational, which the `ring` calls in this file cannot carry as
numerals.  The model used instead is the minimal model of the **quadratic twist
by `37`**, which multiplies those `x`-coordinates by `37` and clears the
denominator while leaving `j` — and hence the row of the table — unchanged.  A
rational cyclic `p`-isogeny is twist-invariant (the kernel of `E^d` is
`C ⊗ χ_d`, still cyclic of order `p` and still Galois-stable), and the consumer
demands only `j(E) = j₀`, so nothing is lost.

## How the two divisibilities are verified

Exactly as at `p ∈ {11, 17, 19}`, and the generic machinery is reused verbatim
from `GenusOneKernel` rather than restated:

* `ΨSq p = preΨ'ₚ ²` for odd `p` (`WeierstrassCurve.ΨSq_ofNat`), so `f ∣ ΨSq 37`
  follows from `f ∣ preΨ'₃₇`, halving the degree before anything else happens.
* `f ∣ preΨ'₃₇` is proven MODULO `f`: the remainders `rₙ := preΨ'ₙ mod f`, all of
  degree `< 18`, are carried along mathlib's own `preNormEDS'` recursion, and the
  chain ends at `r₃₇ = 0`.  **Only seventeen indices are visited** — `1`–`12`,
  `17`–`20` and `37` — because the recursion is a DAG and not a walk: `37`
  reduces to `17, 18, 19, 20`, which reduce to `7`–`12`, which reduce to `1`–`8`.
  Writing the chain for every `n ≤ 37` would be more than twice the work for
  nothing.
* `dvd_multComp` likewise reduces every power `Φ₂^i`, `ΨSq₂^j` modulo `f` before
  multiplying, so each `ring` call is an identity in degree `≤ 36` rather than
  the degree-`72` one the unreduced sum would need.

## Provenance of the constants

The two models and their kernel polynomials were found in PARI/GP (2026-07-30)
as an *untrusted searcher*: `ellisomat(E, 37)` returns the isogeny-degree matrix
`[1, 37; 37, 1]` at both `j`-values, and its isogeny's `h`-component is the
degree-`18` kernel polynomial, confirmed there to divide `elldivpol(E, 37)`.
Every remainder and cofactor below was then computed by exact rational
arithmetic outside Lean.  None of that is a proof: the Lean kernel re-checks
every identity here through `ring` against the explicit witness.
-/
module

